        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Who makes LifeSpan treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              LifeSpan Fitness, an American brand long based in Salt Lake City, sells them; they are manufactured in Taiwan and China. Strength Master Fitness Tech, a Taiwanese manufacturer, presents LifeSpan products in its award listings, and one desk-treadmill review site reported in 2022 that it now owns and runs LifeSpan. We could not confirm the ownership from a primary source.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is LifeSpan the same as Lifespan Fitness Australia?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Lifespan Fitness in Australia is a separate retailer founded in Melbourne in 2007, with its own brands and partnerships with Reebok, Adidas and WalkingPad. Its about page does not mention the American LifeSpan. Australian prices or a .com.au address mean you are looking at the Australian company.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on LifeSpan walking pads?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The TX6-GlowUp is rated for 400 lb by LifeSpan, and independent coverage agrees. LifeSpan lists the TR5000B Pro+ at 400 lb as well, and an independent spec tracker gives the same figure. The TR1200Pro Glow-Up is 330 lb on LifeSpan’s page, although an Amazon listing has claimed 400 lb; plan on 330 lb.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does LifeSpan still sell folding treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not on 14 October 2026. Every folding model on LifeSpan’s US store, from the TR1200i to the TR5500iM, was marked unavailable, as were its light-commercial and commercial treadmills. We found no statement that they are discontinued, so check the store again if you want one, and confirm parts support before buying an older model used.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty do LifeSpan treadmills have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The current under-desk treadmills carry 10 years on the frame, two years on parts and one year on labour, according to LifeSpan’s product pages. No separate motor term is listed. Extended protection plans are sold through a third-party provider. Register the treadmill and keep proof of purchase, as service reports from owners are mixed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a LifeSpan under-desk treadmill worth the price?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For hours of walking every working day, often yes: a 20-inch belt, published deck and duty figures and a 10-year frame warranty are rare among walking pads. For occasional use, a good folding pad costing a fraction as much is the better buy. Weigh the mixed service reports, and confirm stock before you count on a delivery date.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
